  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY NIGHT THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 314 AM CDT Fri Jul 31 2026\n\nThrough Sunday, the overall pattern will remain amplified \nacross the CONUS with a trough anchored from the central Great \nLakes, south southwest into northern Gulf. High pressure will \nremain anchored over the Southwestern CONUS. However, by late \nSunday, a trough will enter the Pacific NW states flattening the\nridge over the northern Rockies and forcing the ridge east into\nsouth central Canada. With the approach of the trough Sunday, \nsurface low pressure and an associated trough will deepen from \ncentral Wyoming into eastern Colorado Sunday. Downstream of this\nfeature, warmer air and gusty southerly winds will push into \nthe forecast area Sunday. Highs will rebound into the 90s, with \nsome lower 100s possible in the eastern panhandle Sunday \nafternoon. With a decent surface pressure gradient across the \nwestern half of the forecast area and h85 winds approaching 15 \nto 20 KTS Sunday afternoon, along with hot temps and minimum RH \nin the range for 15 to 25 percent, am concerned about near \ncritical fire weather conditions generally west of highway 83. \nThe current forecast has min RH around 15 percent in the eastern\npanhandle where highs could top 100 degrees. ATTM fire zone 204\nhas the greatest threat for critical fire weather conditions \nSunday afternoon. Will message this in the HWO with this \nmorning's issuance. On Sunday Night, the models develop a low \nlevel jet from western Kansas into western Nebraska. This will \npostpone, if not totally eliminate, decoupling Sunday night. \nWith winds persisting possibly into the early overnight hours, \nRH recovery Monday morning will be limited to the 45 to 55 \npercent range west of highway 61. The H5 trough will cross over \ninto the northern Rockies Monday forcing a cold front into the \nwestern Dakotas. Downstream of this feature, gusty southerly \nwinds will force warmer air into eastern portions of the \nforecast area. Widespread 90s are probably Monday with readings \npossibly reaching 100 degrees from Garden County east southeast \ninto Lincoln County and points southward. Gusty southerly winds \nahead of the front Monday, will lead to near critical and \npossibly critical fire weather conditions Monday west of highway\n83. Late Monday night the cold front will approach northwestern\nNebraska, making it into central portions of the forecast area \nby daybreak Tuesday (per latest GFS soln). In advance of the \nfront, there will be an increased threat for precipitation \nacross the forecast area Monday night into Tuesday. ATTM, the \ngreatest threat for precipitation appears to be over northern \nportions of the forecast area, where mid level temps in \nassociation with the H5 shortwave trough are coolest. Better \nchances for precipitation in the remainder of the forecast area \nwill arrive Tuesday afternoon into Wednesday. The forecast area \nwill be north of the exiting cold front and precipitation will \nbe driven by a secondary shortwave trough, which emerges from \nWyoming into the Dakotas and northern Nebraska Wednesday. Behind\nthe front, highs will be much cooler for Tuesday with readings \nin the middle 70s over the north to the middle 80s in the south.\nReadings rebound some on Wednesday with a return to 80s and 90s\nfor Thursday.",
